nterstitial_type":"","statuses_count":55684,"translator_type":"none","url":"https://t.co/T5CEcqToIU","want_retweets":false,"withheld_in_countries":[]},"location":{"location":"🚀🏙️"},"media_permissions":{"can_media_tag":false},"parody_commentary_fan_label":"None","profile_image_shape":"Circle","profile_bio":{"description":"Meteorologist / science policy analyst @CFACT | Politically incorrect | Lukewarmer | “The Anti-Greta Thunberg” – NY Post | 📧 cmartz@cfact.org"},"privacy":{"protected":false},"relationship_perspectives":{"following":false},"tipjar_settings":{"is_enabled":false},"verification":{"verified":false}}}},"unmention_data":{},"edit_control":{"edit_tweet_ids":["1781822692607303938"],"editable_until_msecs":"1713658209000","is_edit_eligible":true,"edits_remaining":"5"},"is_translatable":false,"views":{"count":"31998","state":"EnabledWithCount"},"source":"<a href=\"http://twitter.com/download/iphone\" rel=\"nofollow\">Twitter for iPhone</a>","note_tweet":{"is_expandable":true,"note_tweet_results":{"result":{"id":"Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE3ODE4MjI2OTIyMDA0Mzk4MDg=","text":"The global mean surface temperature (GMST) datasets are in unison because they more or less use the same network of stations and there has been collusion between agencies to force the datasets to agree with each other. The fact that all of these datasets that agree with one another isn’t proof that they are correct.\n\nThe linear warming rate of HadCRUT3 began to diverge (lessen) from GISTEMP and NCDC after the year 2000. So, the bias-corrected HadCRUT4 was brought into compliance with the other datasets by 2012, suggesting that the divergence stemmed from a lack of coverage in the Arctic (where most of the warming has occurred) as compared to GISTEMP.\n\nOf course, since the station data used in both datasets is more or less the same, GISTEMP has no better Arctic coverage than HadCRUT3. They are able to claim that since they do an unreasonable extrapolation through area-weighted averaging over the entire Arctic region with only a handful of stations.\n\nOn the satellite front, until 2017, both Remote Sensing Systems (RSS) and the University of Alabama Huntsville (UAH) satellite-derived datasets for the global lower tropospheric temperature (TLT) were in fairly good agreement. Both derive their temperature data from the Advanced Microwave Sounding Units (AMSU) equipped to NASA satellites, but the divergence between the two is a result of how that data is processed.\n\nDr. Carl Mears of RSS came under peer pressure to force fit his satellite-derived TLT data to come into agreement with the GMST datasets, and so with Version 4, the warming rate was increased. UAH aims to remain independent.\n\nThe caption on the RSS site beneath Version 3 read: “𝘕𝘰𝘵𝘦 𝘵𝘩𝘢𝘵 𝘢𝘧𝘵𝘦𝘳 1998, 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘰𝘣𝘴𝘦𝘳𝘷𝘢𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘴 𝘢𝘳𝘦 𝘭𝘪𝘬𝘦𝘭𝘺 𝘵𝘰 𝘣𝘦 𝘣𝘦𝘭𝘰𝘸 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘴𝘪𝘮𝘶𝘭𝘢𝘵𝘦𝘥 𝘷𝘢𝘭𝘶𝘦𝘴, 𝙞𝙣𝙙𝙞𝙘𝙖𝙩𝙞𝙣𝙜 𝙩𝙝𝙖𝙩 𝙩𝙝𝙚 𝙨𝙞𝙢𝙪𝙡𝙖𝙩𝙞𝙤𝙣 𝙖𝙨 𝙖 𝙬𝙝𝙤𝙡𝙚 𝙖𝙧𝙚 𝙥𝙧𝙚𝙙𝙞𝙘𝙩𝙞𝙣𝙜 𝙩𝙤𝙤 𝙢𝙪𝙘𝙝 𝙬𝙖𝙧𝙢𝙞𝙣𝙜” [1]. The caption beneath Version 4 reads: “𝘕𝘰𝘵𝘦 𝘵𝘩𝘢𝘵 𝘢𝘧𝘵𝘦𝘳 1998, 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘰𝘣𝘴𝘦𝘳𝘷𝘢𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯𝘴 𝘢𝘳𝘦 𝘭𝘪𝘬𝘦𝘭𝘺 𝘵𝘰 𝘣𝘦 𝘪𝘯 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘭𝘰𝘸𝘦𝘳 𝘱𝘢𝘳𝘵 𝘰𝘧 𝘵𝘩𝘦 𝘮𝘰𝘥𝘦𝘭 𝘥𝘪𝘴𝘵𝘳𝘪𝘣𝘶𝘵𝘪𝘰𝘯, 𝙞𝙣𝙙𝙞𝙘𝙖𝙩𝙞𝙣𝙜 𝙩𝙝𝙖𝙩 𝙩𝙝𝙚𝙧𝙚 𝙞𝙨 𝙖 𝙨𝙢𝙖𝙡𝙡 𝙙𝙞𝙨𝙘𝙧𝙚𝙥𝙖𝙣𝙘𝙮 𝙗𝙚𝙩𝙬𝙚𝙚𝙣 𝙩𝙝𝙚 𝙢𝙤𝙙𝙚𝙡 𝙥𝙧𝙚𝙙𝙞𝙘𝙩𝙞𝙤𝙣𝙨 𝙖𝙣𝙙 𝙩𝙝𝙚 𝙨𝙖𝙩𝙚𝙡𝙡𝙞𝙩𝙚 𝙤𝙗𝙨𝙚𝙧𝙫𝙖𝙩𝙞𝙤𝙣𝙨” [2].\n\nThe UAH satellite dataset is in closest agreement with independent radiosonde measurements taken from launched weather balloons (Christy et al., 2018) [2]. The UAH satellite and balloon data for the bulk atmosphere warm less than surface observations, which according to climate models, should not be happening in response to greenhouse gas (GHG) forcing.\n\nThe largest GHG warming trend should be in the mid-to-upper troposphere at 200-300 hPa, particularly over the tropics (McKitrick and Christy, 2018) [4]. This is due to enhanced deep tropical  convection (driven by the GHE) which removes heat accumulated at the surface and releases it at high altitudes upon condensation.\n\nThis suggests that much of the surface warming is attributable to urban heat island (UHI) effects, despite claims that it has been “corrected” for through data homogenization methods. The warming is most pronounced over the Northern Hemisphere land areas, fitting the spatial patterns of poor GHCNd station siting and urban heating.\n\n𝗥𝗲𝗳𝗲𝗿𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗲𝘀:\n[1] RSS Climate Analysis (2016): https://t.co/94O8rkWaxI\n\n[2] RSS Climate Analysis (2020): https://t.co/bAT21HSogG\n\n[3] Christy et al. (2018) - Examination of space-based bulk atmospheric temperatures used in climate research: https://t.co/vUJgmifUKG\n\n[4] McKitrick and Christy (2018) - A Test of the Tropical 200- to 300-hPa Warming Rate in Climate Models: https://t.co/TdpV6kbo9Q","entity_set":{"hashtags":[],"symbols":[],"urls":[{"display_url":"web.archive.org/web/2016041317…","expanded_url":"https://web.archive.org/web/20160413173241/https://www.remss.com/research/climate/","url":"https://t.co/94O8rkWaxI","indices":[3137,3160]},{"display_url":"remss.com/research/clima…","expanded_url":"https://www.remss.com/research/climate/","url":"https://t.co/bAT21HSogG","indices":[3195,3218]},{"display_url":"tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.10…","expanded_url":"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01431161.2018.1444293","url":"https://t.co/vUJgmifUKG","indices":[3331,3354]},{"display_url":"agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.10…","expanded_url":"https://agupubs.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1029/2018EA000401","url":"https://t.co/TdpV6kbo9Q","indices":[3462,3485]}],"user_mentions":[]}}}},"grok_analysis_button":true,"quoted_status_result":{"result":{"__typename":"Tweet","rest_id":"1781781841072058523","core":{"user_results":{"result":{"__typename":"User","id":"VXNlcjoxNzIzMDM2Nw==","rest_id":"17230367","affiliates_highlighted_label":{},"avatar":{"image_url":"https://pbs.twimg.com/profile_images/709351023438139392/Hfn0uYR5_normal.jpg"},"core":{"created_at":"Fri Nov 07 12:49:02 +0000 2008","name":"icarus62","screen_name":"icarus62"},"dm_permissions":{"can_dm":false},"follow_request_sent":false,"has_graduated_access":true,"is_blue_verified":false,"legacy":{"de