ely sensitive to greenhouse gases.","entity_set":{"hashtags":[],"symbols":[],"urls":[],"user_mentions":[]},"richtext":{"richtext_tags":[]}}}},"grok_analysis_button":true,"legacy":{"bookmark_count":3,"bookmarked":false,"created_at":"Thu Dec 11 10:40:30 +0000 2025","conversation_id_str":"1999066759240814730","display_text_range":[0,276],"entities":{"hashtags":[],"symbols":[],"timestamps":[],"urls":[],"user_mentions":[]},"favorite_count":18,"favorited":false,"full_text":"Climate sensitivity isn't 3C or 4.5C or even 6C.\n\nClimate sensitivity is 10C. That's the amount of warming caused by a doubling of CO2, which is where we are now (CO2-equivalent).\n\nThe real world includes \"slow\" feedbacks, eg ice sheet albedo and GHG feedbacks. Slow feedbacks","is_quote_status":false,"lang":"en","quote_count":1,"reply_count":1,"retweet_count":5,"retweeted":false,"user_id_str":"2270905347","id_str":"1999066759240814730"},"id":"1999066759240814730","promoted":false},"id":1965}
