,"category":[{"id":713,"name":"Science & Technology","icon_name":"IconBriefcaseStroke"}]},"profile_bio":{"description":"英検1級(2023)IELTS OA 7.5達成(2025)(R8.5L7.0W7.0S7.0)TOEIC970 (2025) TOEFL102 (2025)"},"privacy":{"protected":false},"relationship_perspectives":{"following":false},"tipjar_settings":{"is_enabled":true},"verification":{"verified":false}}}},"unmention_data":{},"edit_control":{"edit_tweet_ids":["1999436788923085023"],"editable_until_msecs":"1765541452000","is_edit_eligible":true,"edits_remaining":"5"},"is_translatable":false,"views":{"count":"62","state":"EnabledWithCount"},"source":"<a href=\"http://twitter.com/download/iphone\" rel=\"nofollow\">Twitter for iPhone</a>","note_tweet":{"is_expandable":true,"note_tweet_results":{"result":{"id":"Tm90ZVR3ZWV0OjE5OTk0MzY3ODU2MDExMTgyMTE=","text":"Climate Change as a Nexus of Science, Politics, and Ethics\n\nClimate change is often framed as an environmental problem driven by rising global temperatures and increasing greenhouse gas emissions. While this scientific description is accurate, it remains incomplete. Climate change is fundamentally an interdisciplinary issue situated at the intersection of natural science, international politics, economics, and moral philosophy. Understanding its complexity requires moving beyond isolated disciplinary perspectives toward an integrated analytical framework.\n\nFrom a scientific standpoint, the mechanisms of climate change are well established. Atmospheric physics demonstrates how carbon dioxide and methane trap heat, leading to long-term climatic shifts. Advances in climate modeling have made it possible to predict regional impacts with increasing precision, from rising sea levels to extreme weather events. However, science alone cannot determine how societies should respond to these findings. Empirical data may explain what is happening, but it does not prescribe what ought to be done.\n\nThis gap between knowledge and action is most evident in the political realm. Climate policy is shaped not only by scientific consensus but also by national interests, power asymmetries, and geopolitical rivalries. Developing countries often argue that industrialized nations bear historical responsibility for emissions, while wealthier states emphasize present and future mitigation efforts. As a result, international negotiations frequently stagnate, revealing how political incentives can undermine collective rationality even in the face of existential threats.\n\nEconomics further complicates the issue by translating environmental damage into financial terms. Cost–benefit analyses attempt to quantify the trade-offs between economic growth and environmental protection. While such models provide valuable insights, critics argue that they systematically undervalue long-term ecological harm and non-market goods such as biodiversity. Reducing the planet’s stability to numerical estimates risks obscuring irreversible losses that cannot be compensated through monetary gains.\n\nEthical considerations bring these tensions into sharper focus. Climate change raises profound questions of intergenerational justice, as the actions of the present impose disproportionate burdens on future generations who lack political representation. Moreover, vulnerable populations—often those least responsible for emissions—are the most exposed to climate-related disasters. This moral asymmetry challenges the adequacy of policy frameworks grounded solely in national sovereignty and economic efficiency.\n\nTechnology is frequently presented as a neutral solution to climate change, offering renewable energy systems, carbon capture technologies, and geoengineering proposals. While technological innovation is indispensable, it is not ethically neutral. Decisions about which technologies to deploy, who controls them, and who bears their risks are inherently political and moral. Overreliance on technological fixes may also foster complacency, delaying necessary changes in consumption patterns and social values.\n\nIn conclusion, climate change cannot be meaningfully addressed through science, politics, economics, or ethics in isolation. It is precisely the interaction among these domains that defines both the problem and its potential solutions. An interdisciplinary perspective reveals that climate change is not merely a technical challenge to be solved, but a test of humanity’s capacity for cooperation, moral responsibility, and long-term thinking.","entity_set":{"hashtags":[],"symbols":[],"urls":[],"user_mentions":[]}}}},"grok_analysis_button":true,"legacy":{"bookmark_count":0,"bookmarked":false,"created_at":"Fri Dec 12 11:10:52 +0000 2025","conversation_id_str":"1999436788923085023","display_text_range":[0,274],"entities":{"hashtags":[],"symbols":[],"timestamps":[],"urls":[],"user_mentions":[]},"favorite_count":0,"favorited":false,"full_text":"Climate Change as a Nexus of Science, Politics, and Ethics\n\nClimate change is often framed as an environmental problem driven by rising global temperatures and increasing greenhouse gas emissions. While this scientific description is accurate, it remains incomplete. Climate","is_quote_status":false,"lang":"en","quote_count":0,"reply_count":0,"retweet_count":0,"retweeted":false,"user_id_str":"1636685016066572288","id_str":"1999436788923085023"},"id":"1999436788923085023","promoted":false},"id":2081}
